  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Promoting an integral management of water resources in Ancash, Peru</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The population of the Community of Cruz de Mayo has clean water for agriculture and subsistence but in the last 20 years, as result of hydroelectric activity and the melting of glaciers, quality and quantity of available water has decreased and conflicts between farmers, agro-industry, mining and hydroelectric companies have grown. 

CAFOD has been working for six years in partnership with the Peruvian Bishops Social Action Commission (CEAS) and Newcastle University in the UK to support the community of Cruz de Mayo to build a solid participatory water monitoring system. This has so far enabled the community to build understanding of local water resources and negotiate with hydroelectric company Duke Energy for a shared use of waters of lake Paron. This new phase of the project (April 2016-March 2018) foresees to consolidate the participatory water monitoring work started previously and foster alternative economic activities that take into account the integral development of the sub-basin Paron-Lullan. Additionally, the community of Cruz de Mayo, in conjunction with key stakeholders and civil society organisations at a local, national and international level, will seek to make contributions to the definition and implementation of public policies for the sustainable management of water resources and their territories.</narrative>
  </description>
